Experiences in the past | The simple past describes completed past events. Regular verbs often add -ed; irregular verbs have forms that must be learned in context. | “Yesterday I visited the library and read a book.” uses visited and read for completed actions. | Change “I walk to school” to a sentence about yesterday. | “I walked to school yesterday.” The time expression and verb agree. | Two friends compare how they spent a school holiday without sharing private location details. | Write three past-tense sentences with an order of events. | Sentences should mark completed actions and keep their timeline consistent.
Recounting an event | A recount tells what happened in time order and may close with a reflection. Sequence words help readers follow events without inventing missing facts. | “First we prepared the room. Then we welcomed visitors. Finally we cleaned up.” shows a clear sequence. | Retell a library visit in three steps using first, then, finally. | An answer should begin with arrival or preparation, continue with a visit activity, and end with departure or reflection. | A class report describes a visit to a museum. Notes contain time, place, and observed exhibits. | Write a short recount from those notes and avoid claiming to have seen unrecorded exhibits. | The recount keeps the documented order and distinguishes observation from personal impression.
Comparing people and things | Comparative adjectives compare two things; superlatives identify one in a group. Use comparisons only when the criterion is clear and respectful. | “This book is longer than that book” compares length; it does not mean the longer book is better. | Compare a 20-page booklet and a 35-page booklet using longer than. | “The 35-page booklet is longer than the 20-page booklet.” The criterion is page count. | A club compares two meeting rooms by capacity and accessibility, not by appearance alone. | Write two comparison sentences with stated criteria. | Each sentence names the criterion, for example “Room A is larger than Room B.”
Invitations and responses | An invitation states activity, time, place, and a way to reply. Replies can accept or decline politely without giving unnecessary personal details. | “Please join our reading circle on Friday at 3 p.m. in the library” gives enough basic information. | Write a polite reply declining an invitation because of a scheduling conflict. | “Thank you for inviting me. I cannot join on Friday.” A reason may be brief. | A class invites another class to a public book exchange and needs confirmation of attendance. | Draft a short invitation with activity, schedule, place, and response request. | The reader can decide whether to attend and knows how to reply; wording remains polite.
Procedures and instructions | A procedure uses ordered action verbs and necessary materials. Readers should be able to follow it safely; missing steps can make a task fail. | “Prepare paper. Fold it in half. Label each side.” is clearer than “make a card somehow.” | Write three English steps to organize a study folder. | Example: “Prepare the folder. Sort papers by topic. Label each section.” | A new student needs instructions to borrow a library book. The process includes asking staff and returning by the due date. | Write a four-step procedure with a final check. | Steps cover choosing, checking out, recording the due date, and returning the book responsibly.
Opinions and reasons | An opinion states a view; a reason explains why. Respectful discussion allows different choices and asks for clarification instead of attacking a person. | “I prefer printed notes because I can mark key words” links an opinion to a reason. | Complete: “I prefer group study because ...” with a relevant reason. | Example: “I prefer group study because friends can explain difficult ideas.” | A club chooses between two reading activities. Members have different preferences and limited time. | Write two opinion sentences and one question to learn another member's reason. | Opinions include reasons; the question invites an explanation without judging the speaker.
